#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100953 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0159149-010c-5bef-a28d-85c4c43f0586" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6479f131-e0ca-5c92-beb1-5b91498b7dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512336} {:answer/id #uuid "853dd5cc-daab-5ece-ae95-a298d6f2b256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512408} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e18d71-cbfe-5d8e-b389-7dcd1319d736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512484} {:answer/id #uuid "af41f8b5-15e8-542f-a34e-a6719d2beff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512509} {:answer/id #uuid "d889c6b3-3044-5c76-99b3-2a2642944a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af41f8b5-15e8-542f-a34e-a6719d2beff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512509} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbc0ee1-4baa-5173-b100-20018e22dddd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "124cf999-fb4b-5e99-b208-917f67eaa2a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-100106" :question/problem "![](images/51/51b119021be889ff440ec31d901187c3ee9072a507336a8e1a1854f729f2734f.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2f(x)}{3{x}^{2}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." :question/worked-solution "Using the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2f(x)}{3{x}^{2}}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(-2f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}3{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=6.$ Calculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}(-2f(x))=-2(6)=-12\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}3{x}^{2}=3(1)^{2}=3.\n$$\n\nTaking the quotient of these limits, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2f(x)}{3{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(-2f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12}{3} \\\\  & =-4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064338 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ce0d18d-f1ee-5654-984d-f191abc9a410" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point Using the Equivalent Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10447d27-bbad-5243-827a-0ec88f52db0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512131} {:answer/id #uuid "6db8fd27-a6bb-5d05-8c7a-556b2db63a39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512359} {:answer/id #uuid "71a944ae-ff58-5447-8134-5585f04d64b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512370} {:answer/id #uuid "b98cc99c-1897-5580-9a45-71a60760d8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512546} {:answer/id #uuid "d6da7332-d0ae-5c57-a840-c9de6058e7d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db8fd27-a6bb-5d05-8c7a-556b2db63a39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512359} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bf2afe7-a005-5071-84b9-bd40024ce506" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d862336-2821-5236-aa0c-5f338ff1395d" :question/math-academy-id "q-102613" :question/problem "Find the instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=3$ by computing the limit of a difference quotient." :question/worked-solution "*Method 1:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(3)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{10(3+h)^{2}-10(3)^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{90+60h+10{h}^{2}-90}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{60h+10{h}^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(60+10h) \\\\  & =60+(0) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Method 2:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10{x}^{2}-10(3)^{2}}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10({x}^{2}-{3}^{2})}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}10(x+3) \\\\  & =10(3+3) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4196423-3401-54e1-b214-9dbc4861cfe1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Spheres, Hemispheres, Cones, and Cylinders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04522f63-eb9b-55fd-8e80-eb04e8eb110e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Oblique cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410205} {:answer/id #uuid "72cbb011-122f-5f3c-b6f3-68935cfd85da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Hemisphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410277} {:answer/id #uuid "8a47e253-b5fe-558a-bcdc-de8e4c29b15e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Oblique cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410288} {:answer/id #uuid "a328348f-8d7e-54d5-861a-7192275e3e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Right cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410314} {:answer/id #uuid "c48304ac-a9cd-5de9-b40a-42e8089a9d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Right cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c48304ac-a9cd-5de9-b40a-42e8089a9d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Right cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410336} :answer-field/id #uuid "22b21cc6-f91d-5265-b595-23f61a650e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "552d9e5c-d038-53c1-b922-9832413afdda" :question/math-academy-id "q-107246" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d6d392d4e0f773f962289075ffa07cd26353ebc00ec50e7473a5c992824417f.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the solid shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The solid is a **right cylinder**. \n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The solid has two parallel circular bases. So, it's a cylinder.\n\n- The axis that passes through the centers of the circles is perpendicular to the bases. So, it's right."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7c84dd5-2c76-5584-81a1-53d2459afb94" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting Horizontal and Vertical Translations of a Given Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27d29f52-682d-5808-8c39-f39fbedb67a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=2,\\,b=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512185} {:answer/id #uuid "52eb9b6f-e01b-50c8-b9fa-d34b7c6dd065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-6,\\,b=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512284} {:answer/id #uuid "67ecaaff-b5ce-51b3-be70-8f364b613256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-2,\\,b=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512347} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4e3141-8e03-58a8-8021-4244dbdf124f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=6,\\,b=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512437} {:answer/id #uuid "ce809b8c-cf9f-5070-93bf-43fcbbf62d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-6,\\,b=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67ecaaff-b5ce-51b3-be70-8f364b613256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-2,\\,b=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512347} :answer-field/id #uuid "dad8af52-393d-59fb-9aa5-60a735e10995" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d94abf8-99d4-59e5-a436-d04813696a03" :question/math-academy-id "q-110144" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d4a5a15c0fc8487aa827a275375c07f048f38ed76fd94b9c40223ff7c38fa9c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$, where $g(x)=f(x+a)+b.$ What are the values of $a$ and $b?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that every point on the graph $y=g(x)$ is two units *to the right* and six units *up* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$ \n\nSo, we conclude that $g(x)=f(x-2)+6,$ and therefore $a=-2$ and $b=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb183013-a42d-50a3-a1b8-cd8501781075" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial With Multiple Variables as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af74e506-fa73-5d0f-bbac-5b9065d0746a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(3m-n)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245880} {:answer/id #uuid "b1441774-55dd-55f5-a7c2-50018f1c1cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(m+n)(m-n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245890} {:answer/id #uuid "b5849601-a466-5b75-bb87-1197a8fd0507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3m-n)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245934} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4d22ea-c702-5d5c-91fc-4f84fea9c229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(3m+n)(3m-n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246144} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd9e8f4-ae35-54c2-875a-ac1c0d9688ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(3m-n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd4d22ea-c702-5d5c-91fc-4f84fea9c229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(3m+n)(3m-n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246144} :answer-field/id #uuid "389cef42-8fe0-5c52-8bba-4a0e05973f3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c434f49e-3ea2-550d-b853-d3c211a71434" :question/math-academy-id "q-112044" :question/problem "Factor the expression $81{m}^{4}-{n}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n81{m}^{4}-{n}^{4}={(9{m}^{2})}^{2}-{({n}^{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{(9{m}^{2})}^{2}-{({n}^{2})}^{2}=(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(9{m}^{2}-{n}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares: \n\n$$\n9{m}^{2}-{n}^{2}={(3m)}^{2}-{n}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(9{m}^{2}-{n}^{2}) & =(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(3m+n)(3m-n)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198352 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829fbee-ca0a-5cf7-b232-c2c6869cb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the First Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01260ea6-923c-58aa-a2e5-5677c1771b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512099} {:answer/id #uuid "652a5ebd-4272-5f28-add3-defc48f2c0b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512340} {:answer/id #uuid "90979a4f-f0a4-5329-9332-c50cc5846e88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512441} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c5fa91-1742-5f01-be92-b24c6d9587a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512538} {:answer/id #uuid "d68c1106-2bb7-5856-b5b7-34531747295e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01260ea6-923c-58aa-a2e5-5677c1771b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512099} :answer-field/id #uuid "21695324-e1d6-52e7-aecd-373a888a2a3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b8bf0b2-6f55-5107-9e2d-cc11b6142890" :question/math-academy-id "q-112414" :question/problem "Find the solution $(a,b)$ to the following system of equations by eliminating the variable $a:$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5a+4b=7 \\\\ -2a+6b=20\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $a$-coefficient in the first equation is $5,$ while in the second equation it is $-2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $5,$ then the $a$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $10$ and $-10,$ and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,5a+4b=7\\,)\\,\\times (2) \\\\ (\\,-2a+6b=20\\,)\\,\\times (5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}10a+8b=14 \\\\ -10a+30b=100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{10a}+8b & =14 \\\\ -\\cancel{10a}+30b & =100 \\\\ (8b+30b) & =(14+100) \\\\ 38b & =114 \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $b=3.$ However, we still need to find $a.$ To do this, we substitute $b=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a+4b & =7 \\\\ 5a+4(3) & =7 \\\\ 5a+12 & =7 \\\\ 5a & =-5 \\\\ a & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-1$ and $b=3,$ which can be written as $(-1,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087856 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed971935-4ef9-5c49-a67d-26dde1a350bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Values of Two Unknowns in a Polynomial Given Two Remainders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f4c8461-b983-5195-b309-a1fd4f447236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512236} {:answer/id #uuid "52ccfcd0-dfef-547c-9767-ca8b5f2e8bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512283} {:answer/id #uuid "8bab351c-3d65-5fe3-9013-2a0e5d4247ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512427} {:answer/id #uuid "8c0e57d9-947f-577b-9e25-695901eeae8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512428} {:answer/id #uuid "9a53dd5a-69f1-5a67-8fc6-fee88e884332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bab351c-3d65-5fe3-9013-2a0e5d4247ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512427} :answer-field/id #uuid "770121c3-2cf2-5300-81b4-eb965c9cabaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5db961df-fac4-59ee-a59e-6595583c0931" :question/math-academy-id "q-112756" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=a{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+bx+1$ has a factor of $(x-1)$ and has a remainder of $4$ when divided by $(x+2).$ Find the value of $a-b.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, it must divide into $f(x)$ with no remainder. In other words, when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-1),$ the remainder is $0.$ It follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =f(1) \\\\  & =a(1)^{3}-2(1)^{2}+b(1)+1 \\\\  & =a-2+b+1 \\\\  & =a+b-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, since we're told that the remainder is $4$ when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+2),$ it follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =f(-2) \\\\  & =a(-2)^{3}-2(-2)^{2}+b(-2)+1 \\\\  & =-8a-8-2b+1 \\\\  & =-8a-2b-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=a+b-1 \\\\ 4=-8a-2b-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying each equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a+b=1 \\\\ 8a+2b=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $b,$ we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ and we add the resulting equation to the second equation. So we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -2a-2b=-2 \\\\ +\\,\\,\\, & \\,8a+2b=-11 \\\\  & \\,6a=-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get $a=-\\frac{13}{6}.$ Finally, we substitute it into $a+b=1$ to find $b=\\frac{19}{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, our final solution is $a-b=-\\frac{16}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3a53bf2-3144-5da3-8ee7-54d1e6c8fd7d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Synthetic Division"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04fbd209-c2ac-5a44-9ef5-0a4c864e35f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512115} {:answer/id #uuid "3481b4a5-7c9b-5a77-bec3-367c925b0f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512215} {:answer/id #uuid "44dd5582-7312-58df-8990-76dca6f6388c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512241} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8e710d-b803-5cd6-87f0-46d6f5f2d623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512318} {:answer/id #uuid "a60d7315-a1fa-59c4-ae95-e9fd8c61350e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44dd5582-7312-58df-8990-76dca6f6388c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512241} :answer-field/id #uuid "3900d287-7003-527b-bb39-f80c78c2331d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b96d00b-58fa-5601-a22a-8b8182429232" :question/math-academy-id "q-112786" :question/problem "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=4$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+32?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=4$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $0$ | $32$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $-8$ | $-32$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-4}={x}^{2}-2x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}-2x-8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-4)(x+2)(x-4) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-4)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=4$ has multiplicity $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba5bf1d9-f6e2-5494-91fd-31c20d7fa09b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Rectangular Solid: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05308cdc-72ad-53cb-8842-72a8ffd93c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$400$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512116} {:answer/id #uuid "2422f0ee-d558-5812-be60-ff74a4a72d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$900$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512179} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc997cf-68a9-5c5a-a91f-44f31f08abc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$800$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512207} {:answer/id #uuid "d7435a38-2651-52af-beed-87c2b38bfbff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$500$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512611} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5e551c-a4be-5e65-a87d-376cd2db9ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$450$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2422f0ee-d558-5812-be60-ff74a4a72d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$900$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512179} :answer-field/id #uuid "45a670a5-6eca-551c-9bba-97922b332700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "751e0aa2-a931-559b-9a2f-5250360d1346" :question/math-academy-id "q-118876" :question/problem "A company rents ten identical storage units in the form of a rectangular prism. Each unit is $5\\,\\text{yd}$ long, $3\\,\\text{yd}$ wide, and $3\\text{yd}$ high. If it costs $$2$ per cubic yard per day to rent a storage unit, how much does it cost the company each day to rent the storage units?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the volume of one storage unit.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=5,\\,w=3,\\,h=3\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=5\\cdot 3\\cdot 3=45\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe total volume of the ten units is\n\n$$\n45\\cdot 10=450\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to rent the storage units, each day the company must pay\n\n$$\n450\\cdot $2=$900.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbb5348e-e292-513f-8b0a-90428692fc0a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Probability of the Complement Given the Probability of the Event"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12870f9a-8af7-5922-9f55-72018b822cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512139} {:answer/id #uuid "522d1120-93d4-5cbb-a277-c571fccf616d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512280} {:answer/id #uuid "a20177b3-3f04-5b8a-9227-27b704dc29d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512476} {:answer/id #uuid "b237eebf-9c43-59fb-899a-a30b67926304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512519} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa309f2-83c5-59e0-83a5-90aeb8dbb22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b237eebf-9c43-59fb-899a-a30b67926304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512519} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbd4dbe8-7797-5473-8b71-522ba839d039" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f9cab85-d551-5a63-8c24-8e21e5ba12ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-119398" :question/problem "![](images/79/791a76ebbb34751b2ce345243c5de4baf95a32357ce48830e3ea43043011b5e1.png)\n\nGiven the above sample space, find $P({X}^{′}).$" :question/worked-solution "The Venn diagram tells us that $P(X)=\\frac{2}{9}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}^{′}) & =1-P(X) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b11642dd-80cc-539c-8c2d-8cdee58e9d3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector in a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bf6d318-67a3-589f-aa06-712a4c979552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512262} {:answer/id #uuid "5466b133-5ea5-525f-8cd7-1a549d321d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512288} {:answer/id #uuid "8a9a8734-2fa1-5cd5-97d6-c6ffcae6ba4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512426} {:answer/id #uuid "9b35183a-e748-54ed-8dd3-0bcbe740b891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512466} {:answer/id #uuid "ed844729-fa98-5650-835d-6732a8a4b167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b35183a-e748-54ed-8dd3-0bcbe740b891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512466} :answer-field/id #uuid "5749cf60-a3f1-5876-9c2b-03d5d66464f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "45f48cf6-e519-5407-abc6-4bbd59c8789a" :question/math-academy-id "q-119805" :question/problem "![](images/95/95c30eab094a24beb6976262167401efa973dbac12781c69d074eb14e12e2743.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $m+n.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n:$ \n\n![](images/87/87647211f77776c5f5f740c205fafb150eef1b9d3ebefb82df73a9fdcd953bdb.png)\n\nIn $△AHB$, we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=0.5=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $BH=2.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|m+n| & =AB \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.$ Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073674 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1ec0eb3-ea8e-5783-b6a2-dc7128709f71" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Trigonometric Ratio With a Linear Expression Angle as a Cofunction of a Complementary Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73514f22-423e-5c6d-b7a6-f17968101937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta +{28}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512371} {:answer/id #uuid "bcef4960-4cea-5205-b924-fe432441044d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡({28}^{∘}-\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512552} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fd120c-71e9-5eb5-a646-d616f0a05430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡({28}^{∘}-\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512569} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b86ad8-0c94-5ca9-9616-ccb1959bddfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\theta +{28}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512584} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e51dd2-4683-56b2-b1fe-dba2254dfac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta +{28}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcef4960-4cea-5205-b924-fe432441044d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡({28}^{∘}-\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512552} :answer-field/id #uuid "6df2773c-0b27-5748-b7a3-895628516a65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9a8f3f4-0ef5-5e5b-92f7-76c0a9db8aab" :question/math-academy-id "q-121632" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta +{62}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "We know that the cofunction of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and the angle complementary to $\\theta +{62}^{∘}$ is\n\n$$\n{90}^{∘}-(\\theta +{62}^{∘})={90}^{∘}-\\theta -{62}^{∘}={28}^{∘}-\\theta .\n$$\n\n So we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta +{62}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({90}^{∘}-(\\theta +{62}^{∘}))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({28}^{∘}-\\theta ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1740fa9-50b1-5fad-9f99-31820dd77067" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Radius or Diameter of a Circle Given the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e42b42-afd8-540d-8e7d-f99d2145d341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512102} {:answer/id #uuid "86c44bd0-2362-59c6-bcd8-9f87233418ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{25\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512410} {:answer/id #uuid "981ccea8-d871-51f5-b71f-5cf2249a16cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512457} {:answer/id #uuid "b180e177-b74d-52e3-b883-4d3735cac1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512517} {:answer/id #uuid "e93f025c-4fe3-5087-9fca-73eabb8d7f1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e93f025c-4fe3-5087-9fca-73eabb8d7f1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512658} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d1c702d-6a7e-53a6-b332-1229b7e63178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd94009d-7285-58c6-9df1-2341d32131ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-122326" :question/problem "What is the diameter of a circle if its area is $\\frac{9}{25}\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of a circle is $A=\\pi {r}^{2}$, where $r$ is the radius. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{9}{25}\\pi  & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{9}{25}\\cancel{\\pi } & =\\cancel{\\pi }{r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diameter is\n\n$$\nd=2\\cdot r=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2e28247-1113-5d9f-9c76-49af8b498fc5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio of a Quadrantal Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193f007a-0943-5ee4-b21d-1c12d8a9aa4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512156} {:answer/id #uuid "346c7c5f-30ec-593f-bea5-24e469b9441b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512214} {:answer/id #uuid "654bd16e-0fb9-537f-a166-1c6dfab5eb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512341} {:answer/id #uuid "7580ba1f-3abd-5900-907d-0ec7cc95376e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512376} {:answer/id #uuid "a6855aa1-9c74-5c2d-8f0f-04a41e81ebd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7580ba1f-3abd-5900-907d-0ec7cc95376e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512376} :answer-field/id #uuid "5809f205-7813-519f-9ba7-8f909067fdec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55febbab-96d0-5d10-a73b-1109a9b31684" :question/math-academy-id "q-123586" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡0+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The angles $0$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $0.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡0=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡0+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1+1=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b04804-a1f3-57de-b966-65074210fc6b" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Product or Quotient of Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0250596b-bb74-53f3-9efe-07422f328129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512104} {:answer/id #uuid "57e21c9d-b9e5-5edc-bdb6-a68449e17988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512299} {:answer/id #uuid "8a363189-ada6-5f18-817b-14cb05549c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512423} {:answer/id #uuid "deb59f15-474e-5cf9-aa95-d55b21ee935f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512634} {:answer/id #uuid "dfcff7a0-7ab3-58d4-827e-87b601adc05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57e21c9d-b9e5-5edc-bdb6-a68449e17988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512299} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e1969a8-d004-5594-8163-9a16bf27541a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef24e72f-f752-5e87-a5ef-c5be48597b10" :question/math-academy-id "q-133428" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are **irrational**?\n\nI. ${8}^{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{36}$\n\nII. ${3}^{20}\\cdot 7.0523$\n\nIII. $\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {2}^{-15}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- the product of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Expression I is rational. Notice that $\\sqrt{36}=6$ is a rational number. The number ${8}^{5}$ is rational too. So, their product must be rational.\n\n- Expression II is rational. Since both ${3}^{20}$ and $7.0523$ are rational, their product must be rational.\n\n- Expression III is irrational. Since $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational and ${2}^{-15}$ is rational, their product must be irrational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176035 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa97e57a-70d8-5708-8b7d-fa05604a3f88" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Residual Plots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01dedd08-ded8-5ff9-8bc9-adeb91c83949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512101} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5cec74-241f-5ece-ab07-506d78ba15ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512390} {:answer/id #uuid "8627579e-91b5-5b56-a03e-7f43967fceb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512409} {:answer/id #uuid "904444cb-2f64-5eb5-9c3b-5a0b71fe680b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512440} {:answer/id #uuid "ce895cca-e4ca-58bc-9b7e-d4816e68a767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01dedd08-ded8-5ff9-8bc9-adeb91c83949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512101} :answer-field/id #uuid "a743042b-63eb-545c-bdc0-1aab409141d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "510cace3-4d24-50e7-9ee2-e819b9c603a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-134815" :question/problem "![](images/ce/cec25c6ab182494771824bae8c2182bf38b57e2d1e81d913e3e49916c8cc3d60.png)\n\nThe residual plot above was constructed after fitting a linear regression model on a set of bivariate numerical data $(x,y).$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. When $x=8,$ the regression equation overestimates the value of $y.$\n\nII. The residuals appear to be randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\n\nIII. This linear regression is probably a good fit for the data." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The residual equals the actual value minus the estimated value:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the plot, the residual at $x=8$ is negative. So, we have that $\\text{Actual}<\\text{Estimated},$ and therefore the model overestimates  this data point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The plot shows the residuals tend to decrease as $x$ increases. So, there is a trend, as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0bb186e7b1c1923e7e8b2776b202b1dcd9b930c56583199964e47530805d246d.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the residuals are *not* randomly scattered above and below the horizontal axis, our regression line is *not* a good fit for the given data.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e8b25b3-5cc4-51b7-8ff9-fc1773797ad4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "227b5733-3404-5771-9605-7aa40c072b17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410227} {:answer/id #uuid "9a1dad57-e593-551e-8c2e-8e04cb1031ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410301} {:answer/id #uuid "9baac897-f09d-582f-b1da-3a9c62f28f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410302} {:answer/id #uuid "ad391d1e-b265-5dea-b25b-f4b234954d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410321} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c024e0-14f1-55cd-b9c7-b885fa85f645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9baac897-f09d-582f-b1da-3a9c62f28f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410302} :answer-field/id #uuid "7011823c-cf5d-51f9-9bd3-0486581bd8c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "340eeb23-68bd-5b4a-ac71-e640f063960b" :question/math-academy-id "q-13589" :question/problem "If $f(x)=-2x+1$ and $g(x)=-3x-7,$ then $(f-g)(x)=$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f-g)(x)$ means the difference of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\nTo find the difference of the two functions, we subtract $g(x)$ from $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =f(x)-g(x) \\\\  & =(-2x+1)-(-3x-7) \\\\  & =(-2x-(-3x))+(1-(-7)) \\\\  & =x+8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184227 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c76594da-4831-56c4-8c49-68349ba62a19" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Several Transformations: Horizontal Stretch Followed By Horizontal Shift Inside Parentheses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b707a1d1-57fa-5736-811f-bf2ae96d060d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8f(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512537} {:answer/id #uuid "c39f5368-56a8-56a6-9249-778f2951044b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512567} {:answer/id #uuid "ce83393b-e04b-54a4-bf3c-968bb6cbc7d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8f(5x)+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512595} {:answer/id #uuid "ead58f74-c494-532c-8715-c931593791f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8f(\\frac{x}{5})+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512664} {:answer/id #uuid "fed01fe7-79b8-5045-81a9-f0e7af4fb8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{8}f(\\frac{x}{5}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c39f5368-56a8-56a6-9249-778f2951044b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512567} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffa36a73-cb38-5a3e-b22f-94ae7972e4d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d424faa-a830-5c69-a1d8-32908ad96638" :question/math-academy-id "q-141752" :question/problem "Given the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following transformations in the order given:\n\n1\\. Stretch it by a scale factor of $8$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n2\\. Stretch it by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n3\\. Translate it to the left by $10$ units\n\nWhat is the final function expression after applying all the transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To find the expression, let's apply each of the transformations in turn.\n\n1\\. Stretching the graph of $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $8$ parallel to the $y$-axis is equivalent to multiplying the function expression by $8.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(x).\n  $$\n\n2\\. Stretching the graph of $y=8f(x)$ by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $\\frac{1}{5}x.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(\\frac{1}{5}x)=8f(\\frac{x}{5}).\n  $$\n\n3\\. Translating the graph of $y=8f(\\frac{x}{5})$ by $10$ units to the left is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $(x+10).$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(\\frac{1}{5}(x+10))=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2).\n  $$\n\nTherefore, the final function expression is $y=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b6b70eb-df57-5d17-a753-645ccdf94945" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of an Endpoint Given the Midpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41846390-6a52-578d-8160-9a5ffe51b3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410247} {:answer/id #uuid "524f4e0d-a8f4-5b13-89de-967398ab31ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410260} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7dda1e-729e-5cf8-8a43-96eadd48414d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410263} {:answer/id #uuid "6a71db0b-e656-5269-a146-a69fd7acd33c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410272} {:answer/id #uuid "95aeaeed-6ae9-5f05-9597-b35749ede8a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "524f4e0d-a8f4-5b13-89de-967398ab31ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410260} :answer-field/id #uuid "385783ed-c2b9-5782-a389-7faefeb3d5c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd717350-e4af-57c0-a511-28912f1d1ece" :question/math-academy-id "q-14365" :question/problem "If the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(7,-4)$ and $(a,b)$ is $(2,1),$\nthen $a+b=$" :question/worked-solution "The given points are ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(2,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (2,1) & =(\\frac{7+a}{2},\\frac{-4+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=\\frac{7+a}{2} \\\\ 1=\\frac{-4+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}a=-3 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(-3,6)$ and $a+b=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7266d5c7-67b9-59db-b994-07dcb143af1f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Simple Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64a8b4ab-c23e-5d88-b637-6f5584a95608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+5}{x+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512338} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9ca4df-8417-5084-ac48-203c262cbbfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{7-6x}{5x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512356} {:answer/id #uuid "8a28b1aa-9eaa-5f37-9ff3-545ca9a7ab3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{6-x}{x-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512422} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a1b1d8-acbe-5be7-a6ee-b82eda6bf9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{7x+6}{x-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512490} {:answer/id #uuid "dce17946-5ca5-50b7-9337-491e29e13717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{6-7x}{x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dce17946-5ca5-50b7-9337-491e29e13717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{6-7x}{x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512629} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec781e0d-5672-5d8a-a7d3-0b30ab64cffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09439e98-9680-5cbd-876f-1f24d85d22d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-148512" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{6-5x}{x+7}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6-5x}{x+7}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{6-5y}{y+7}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{6-5y}{y+7} \\\\ x(y+7) & =6-5y \\\\ xy+7x & =6-5y \\\\ xy+5y & =6-7x \\\\ y(x+5) & =6-7x \\\\ y & =\\frac{6-7x}{x+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{6-7x}{x+5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054733 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a09b3f4a-a1df-504e-8e9f-510225da1392" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Two Complex Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e6c14c3-46ef-52b6-9c5a-9d55e3141b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81+15\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512271} {:answer/id #uuid "60b74954-3003-503f-a9dc-2f3cea1add5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63+15\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512330} {:answer/id #uuid "60e0e66a-c149-588b-90b5-4254cd022492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-67-15\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512331} {:answer/id #uuid "68ab3fb9-a119-597e-9344-6ff98a17ba94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63+12\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512351} {:answer/id #uuid "7e36ff1c-756d-5ab9-a64f-f67db129bbaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81-12\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e6c14c3-46ef-52b6-9c5a-9d55e3141b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81+15\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512271} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc5ff01-f7ae-5810-8e9a-c63f6da9b11b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4508476-b89c-53c0-90e9-f3a9b08e0f33" :question/math-academy-id "q-16295" :question/problem "$(6-9\\,\\text{i})(3+7\\,\\text{i})=$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying two complex numbers is similar to multiplying two binomials. We also use the fact that ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6-9\\,\\text{i})(3+7\\,\\text{i}) & =6\\cdot 3+6\\cdot 7\\,\\text{i}+(-9\\,\\text{i})\\cdot 3+(-9\\,\\text{i})\\cdot 7\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =18+42\\,\\text{i}-27\\,\\text{i}-63\\,{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =18+15\\,\\text{i}-63\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =18+15\\,\\text{i}+63 \\\\  & =81+15\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "874dfb35-398e-5ed7-8a76-f0011569e6a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Complement Using a Venn Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1537842d-13cc-58e0-a0d1-143d9983a915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512145} {:answer/id #uuid "25d9c9bd-9d1a-5474-8d96-6dd5e36e339b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512181} {:answer/id #uuid "48992171-11ab-56d9-b3bd-6dc0be5319e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512255} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd7ce66-80c6-5c47-a014-bc47fefa20f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512396} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4ece7b-d879-59f6-b3c7-36a00c82fb05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25d9c9bd-9d1a-5474-8d96-6dd5e36e339b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512181} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f94bd53-f137-5cf0-9547-b2c16a22c866" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81a4737e-d96b-5774-b418-c2e889c0a2b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-180277" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a61a6232b2041bab3d455a7d765311ddfae25f674f8dbbf79696d2137355b159.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram shown above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space.\n\nGiven that every outcome is equally likely, find $P({A}^{′}\\cup {B}^{′}).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, ${A}^{′}\\cup {B}^{′}$ is represented by the area outside the circle $B$ or outside the circle $A$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/6a/6a3bb7b671e45aeb15d3239cae6fcedb7c36e52b15141d66b23e65066ab37edc.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in ${A}^{′}\\cup {B}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\n2+5+1=8\n$$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n2+9+5+1=17.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}^{′}\\cup {B}^{′})=\\frac{8}{17}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57bae420-d529-5b9d-a306-37127c523be6" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Rule of Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f9be4f9-9349-5fa3-b613-a61f24179452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "270" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410223} {:answer/id #uuid "8697752e-5619-5132-bce4-85f1b8da875e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "248" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410285} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1dbebb-e0c2-52a0-8350-bb215802044b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410306} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e38e02-fb12-5161-9fd0-deab260ddeca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "310" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410316} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1bc7e2-fa14-5ac8-9514-f051895c965d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce1bc7e2-fa14-5ac8-9514-f051895c965d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410345} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b3319b-2af6-598f-8941-02f6c4fc8ef7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9db0cb85-c477-5f32-9094-207566837840" :question/math-academy-id "q-19821" :question/problem "Peter has $8$ shirts, $6$ pants, and $6$ pairs of shoes. If Peter wears one shirt, one pant, and one pair of shoes, how many different ways can Peter dress?" :question/worked-solution "Using the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of outfits} & =\\text{Number of shirts} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of pants} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of pairs of shoes} \\\\  & =8\\times 6\\times 6 \\\\  & =288.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Peter can dress in $288$ different ways."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054679 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0996a6f2-3c15-5670-891a-34fd6ecc8b72" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Variance or Standard Deviation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50cba50f-0215-54ad-96b6-a3d381b47f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50cba50f-0215-54ad-96b6-a3d381b47f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512277} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0a16f09-2e74-5931-9828-588ba4cfaa5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac5ee09a-446b-5918-96fe-4cc239c6a7e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-254742" :question/problem "Consider the frequency table below for a continuous variable $x.$ The total number of observations is $n=20,$ and the estimated sample mean is $\\overset{―}{x}≈13.5.$\n\n| Range | Frequency $({f}_{i})$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0\\le x<10$ | $8$ |\n| $10\\le x<20$ | $7$ |\n| $20\\le x<30$ | $5$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nUsing the midpoint of each class interval to approximate all observations within that class, estimate the standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{n},$ using $n$ as the denominator in the variance formula.\n\nRounded to one decimal place, ${\\sigma }_{n}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a frequency table containing continuous data split into $K$ classes, we can estimate the variance by treating all observations within each class as though they were equal to the midpoint of that class. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{n}^{2}≈\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{K}{f}_{i}\\cdot ({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${f}_{i}$ is the frequency of the $i$th class,\n\n- ${m}_{i}$ is the midpoint of the $i$th class,\n\n- $n$ is the total number of observations, and\n\n- $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the estimated mean, calculated using the class midpoints.\n\nTo find the variance, we add columns to our table for the midpoints of each interval, the squared deviations of the midpoints from the mean, and the products of the squared deviations and frequencies.\n\n| Range | ${f}_{i}$ | ${m}_{i}$ | ${({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}$ | ${f}_{i}{({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0\\le x<10$ | $8$ | $5$ | $72.25$ | $578$ |\n| $10\\le x<20$ | $7$ | $15$ | $2.25$ | $15.75$ |\n| $20\\le x<30$ | $5$ | $25$ | $132.25$ | $661.25$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, we divide the sum of all the values in the last column by $20$ (the total number of observations):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{n}^{2} & ≈\\frac{578+15.75+661.25}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1255}{20} \\\\  & =62.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the estimated standard deviation is the square root of the estimated variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{n} & ≈\\sqrt{62.75}≈7.9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068831 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046339ca-d635-57b1-a4b9-a7619ba24dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512111} {:answer/id #uuid "1eededde-27c4-5ab6-921a-69c922b3ddd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512163} {:answer/id #uuid "4846d2a5-0437-5027-a18c-e8704132c534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512253} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc266e6-456a-5c58-bbb2-5385368780aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512431} {:answer/id #uuid "ec722ee2-a7c4-5b69-bb9e-dbec3b88f301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec722ee2-a7c4-5b69-bb9e-dbec3b88f301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512672} :answer-field/id #uuid "da30e9ce-0a25-5add-9fa0-a7b33a5bb2d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ad05a96-8f5a-585b-894f-27d371577458" :question/math-academy-id "q-27541" :question/problem "![](images/41/41875c5ab1216b78760c1c2dddec6ffc31bfd57d2da27f4797b1190dbf4af709.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$ Hence, $c=-3.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-3 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}-3 & =1 \\\\ k-3 & =1 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=4-(-3)=7.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213005 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46822f28-8863-5150-a420-09c61a5c28b1" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Factored Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e944915-2fc2-5b59-b074-8518099067d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e944915-2fc2-5b59-b074-8518099067d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512398} :answer-field/id #uuid "9df0f739-7e6e-51a0-aeca-cefca94dcd0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "19e31d5d-5933-52fe-a699-d3240dcd05a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-288661" :question/problem "The equation\n\n$$\n(ax-8)(x+b)=4{x}^{2}-32x+48\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants. What is the value of $a+b?$\n\n$a+b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the product on the left-hand side must expand to match the right-hand side.\n\nFirst, let's expand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(ax-8)(x+b) & =a{x}^{2}+abx-8x-8b \\\\  & =a{x}^{2}+(ab-8)x-8b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we equate the expanded form to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+(ab-8)x-8b=4{x}^{2}-32x+48.\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the coefficients of corresponding terms.\n\n- Equating the coefficients of ${x}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\na=4.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constant terms, we get\n\n$$\n-8\\cdot b=48⇒b=-6.\n$$\n\nLet's verify that values $a=4$ and $b=-6$ produce the correct linear coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ab-8 & =4\\cdot (-6)-8 \\\\  & =-24-8 \\\\  & =-32\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the coefficient of $x$ on the right-hand side. So, our solution is valid.\n\nFinally, we compute the value of $a+b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =4+(-6) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "410a9971-d4c0-57ff-b936-58621a2313b5" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Functions With Alternative Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043178c1-c125-508a-9bc7-2d3b5ab2bbaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(t)=3t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512110} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc7d993-8b07-5c1f-bb87-a59ad81eac7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(t)=5t-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512392} {:answer/id #uuid "be0ba7e5-fe56-50ea-afc4-f6da042055f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(t)=5t-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512555} {:answer/id #uuid "c7de47a7-d842-546b-a5f0-ba929b208c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(t)=3t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512582} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9abff9-7b86-525c-b8a8-762f29c56795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(t)=5t+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bc7d993-8b07-5c1f-bb87-a59ad81eac7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(t)=5t-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512392} :answer-field/id #uuid "456409ce-9cbe-59f6-9c9f-5b1ca5269b70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18899ab8-1919-5955-a40e-8e20033f8007" :question/math-academy-id "q-293604" :question/problem "A generator consumes $3$ liters of fuel per hour for the first two hours of use and $5$ liters for each additional hour. Which function $F$ gives the total amount of fuel consumed after $t$ hours, where $t\\ge 2?$" :question/worked-solution "The total amount of fuel consumed consists of two parts.\n\n- For the first $2$ hours, the generator consumes\n\n$$\n3\\times 2=6\\,\\,\\text{liters}.\n$$\n\n- For each additional hour beyond the first $2,$ the generator consumes\n\n$$\n5(t-2)\\,\\,\\text{liters}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total amount of fuel $F$ consumed after $t$ hours, where $t\\ge 2,$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =6+5(t-2) \\\\  & =6+5t-10 \\\\  & =5t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30abea2b-3b0e-5d3f-88c3-deb7c9ada4c8" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Using Linear Equations With Two Variable Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26b1ddc1-9435-5f7f-b5dd-42b065875b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512183} {:answer/id #uuid "8d542712-a993-5adf-89b6-a680649fc5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512430} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ea0f46-6409-5ea6-87af-ac98474c7f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512479} {:answer/id #uuid "a388a803-84bd-53e8-aa65-d13f7f96e425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512482} {:answer/id #uuid "f32cdaeb-1435-578d-beb3-bd4b850bf04a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a388a803-84bd-53e8-aa65-d13f7f96e425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512482} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe515d97-b46e-5b25-875a-5d4729fa788a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "671acf01-53e4-5ebe-ba29-cc75ccac1d67" :question/math-academy-id "q-3414" :question/problem "Caden sold half of his comic books and then bought sixteen more. If he now has $36$, how many comic books did he have at the start?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x=$ the number of comic books Caden had at the start.\n\nWe note that:\n\n- Half the number of comic books corresponds to $\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n- The number of comic books remaining after selling half of them corresponds to $x-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n- The number of comic books remaining after selling half and buying sixteen more corresponds to $x-\\frac{1}{2}x+16.$\n\nThe final number of comic books is $36,$ so we have the equation\n\n$$\nx-\\frac{1}{2}x+16=36.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{1}{2}x+16 & =36 \\\\ (1-\\frac{1}{2})x+16 & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+16 & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+16-16 & =36-16 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =20 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\ x & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, Caden had $40$ comic books at the start."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50de296d-56a1-5166-aa7d-42de027a33bc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trigonometry to Find an Angle in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fd2d8ba-3ba8-5ea6-a35d-4969c52ff9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512275} {:answer/id #uuid "531e8eab-af68-5079-9c20-857bd006a3f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512285} {:answer/id #uuid "64f794dc-e2dc-5758-ad2d-f07b9dd4ce38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512339} {:answer/id #uuid "cefdb48d-3425-56ca-aa9f-081a9420b77e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512597} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1ded3b-66b2-5d93-9cde-d2879d7ff545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fd2d8ba-3ba8-5ea6-a35d-4969c52ff9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512275} :answer-field/id #uuid "9707ee33-50e5-5d83-8659-e34d49a8a2a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a8a7118-dcfe-5423-982d-0747799e649d" :question/math-academy-id "q-34279" :question/problem "![](images/09/09ea9b27d573f7ca318e00a912a18d6c8de8b66ead5c85fb90ebea7f5bd20ca8.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(2\\sqrt{3},-6),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the angle that is formed with the $x$-axis such that ${\\theta }^{′}+\\theta =2\\pi $\n\n![](images/eb/eb659af0765e200333ca35b716772d92765c7f46ccc4b267243b3c82ba88a1e8.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{6}{2\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\theta +{\\theta }^{′}=2\\pi ,$ we get $\\theta =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50de296d-56a1-5166-aa7d-42de027a33bc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trigonometry to Find an Angle in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048aac5a-0f26-5bb2-8fc3-e1525f3c52a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{220}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512112} {:answer/id #uuid "07fc1d0e-4b4b-5938-884e-27a158d44eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{200}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512122} {:answer/id #uuid "1453fd1b-3ee6-55e4-87aa-1fe26fc47d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{210}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512142} {:answer/id #uuid "33f89d2b-d7fe-5ded-8d87-7f20aa7f2159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{190}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512213} {:answer/id #uuid "659ef10a-f223-514c-a277-cdc5f9c36beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{230}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1453fd1b-3ee6-55e4-87aa-1fe26fc47d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{210}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512142} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eecf4b6-4798-5f08-9a2c-b96119d1c2e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e50efe9-568f-54f5-835e-03a6e09d3518" :question/math-academy-id "q-34313" :question/problem "![](images/23/23f0dba237502bc7261fd8f28327beefd5872bb4b0f503930c488b2857fcef24.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(-\\sqrt{3},-1),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the acute angle that is formed with the $x$-axis such that ${\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘}=\\theta $\n\n![](images/d1/d14f62179f831ebdc177eac7bd2899679864305d4104d4b61f304eb4fa9e133d.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\theta ={\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘},$ we get $\\theta ={30}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={210}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa5d982f-ff13-5a31-9cd3-d5847ea2790c" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing a Binomial Within the Radical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69df2f53-5c50-59ae-8338-33f06671b49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{y-3}-10}{y-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512353} {:answer/id #uuid "6c32fdae-9578-5230-9c6a-0030dc42ee0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{y+3}+10}{y-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512357} {:answer/id #uuid "97224d93-ee75-5b28-87bf-a215b4d67a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{y+3}-10}{y+7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512455} {:answer/id #uuid "c408e350-5f98-5e07-beed-90e315ab7e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{y+3}+2}{y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512571} {:answer/id #uuid "eb76173e-aa86-561d-80e6-1b55db1b9cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{y-3}+2}{y-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c32fdae-9578-5230-9c6a-0030dc42ee0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{y+3}+10}{y-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512357} :answer-field/id #uuid "767f1526-f555-548a-8c1f-53ab5b15fedc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8bdad77-9abf-5122-8282-321bd75ae69b" :question/math-academy-id "q-34858" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{y+3}-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{y+3}+2.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{y+3}+2,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{y+3}-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{y+3}-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{y+3}+2}{\\sqrt{y+3}+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5(\\sqrt{y+3}+2)}{(\\sqrt{y+3}-2)(\\sqrt{y+3}+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{y+3}+10}{(\\sqrt{y+3})^{2}-{2}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{y+3}+10}{y+3-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{y+3}+10}{y-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19695af-0879-5a00-b400-1c4a490f7bfa" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15d8ad0d-a6a9-5c9d-8b4e-ece065c13c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512148} {:answer/id #uuid "389fc1a2-a5ba-57bb-b0b8-1e8ad5cb1eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512224} {:answer/id #uuid "3c92e62b-f9d1-5567-ac76-513d8a2c9e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512231} {:answer/id #uuid "7a92d27e-54b0-5d4c-9818-cfe70961fad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512389} {:answer/id #uuid "e9bb5d36-6be0-565e-a094-366239849940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a92d27e-54b0-5d4c-9818-cfe70961fad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512389} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5da9fec-9cf5-5ef3-8a64-44b81b106bd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0821d791-2798-561e-8237-7b4aabdd155c" :question/math-academy-id "q-35003" :question/problem "![](images/06/06e792c5b74eda036b55ac3728122737da22c9824f89f982b50657ab07391269.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x),$ and hence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As $x$ approaches $1$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x),$ and hence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\n We can't say that the limits are equal because we can't compare undefined values.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b01281fd-5272-579c-98d5-ca5cc00c73e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Maps Presented in Function Notation That Are Rigid Motions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11836738-7848-544f-964f-d18900f41ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512136} {:answer/id #uuid "39a67398-ddde-57d6-9d3c-e46cbe169db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512225} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9e5c61-489e-5929-8866-74f1bcafce96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512315} {:answer/id #uuid "a3469b93-f669-5fdc-b195-9fe95068770f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512481} {:answer/id #uuid "d5efbd0f-bd64-5ab7-99fc-db3c57d526db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11836738-7848-544f-964f-d18900f41ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512136} :answer-field/id #uuid "d57c22b9-eb8a-53fc-aa35-99d38f580f0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7b38bd8-148f-55bd-b6dc-69c04541be76" :question/math-academy-id "q-45468" :question/problem "Which of the following transformations map every polygon to a congruent polygon?\n\nI. $(x,y)↦(x-1,y)$\n\nII. $(x,y)↦(-y,x)$\n\nIII. $(x,y)↦(2x,y)$" :question/worked-solution "A rigid motion is a transformation that preserves distances. There are three types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n\n- Rotations\n\n- Reflections\n\nAlso, two polygons are congruent if and only if one can be mapped to another using a combination of rigid motions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is a translation of $1$ unit to the right. This is a rigid motion. So, it maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n\n- Transformation II is a rotation of ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about the origin. This is a rigid motion. So, it maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n\n- Transformation III is a stretch with a stretch factor of $2$ in the $x$-direction. This is *not* a rigid motion. So, it does not map every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a23a10c1-a091-55bb-98a6-7718e46d841b" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Parabola With Leading Coefficients in Vertex Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dc2ec08-ccd3-52d6-ba27-4d0c5b4a6f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512203} {:answer/id #uuid "3be7a489-8098-540d-bf9d-af04ee7bcc91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512229} {:answer/id #uuid "481a228a-6054-5d3b-9727-76814a95d100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512252} {:answer/id #uuid "5882ed18-1e8f-5fb9-ae77-3f8984bbac2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512304} {:answer/id #uuid "d79197b3-4c94-57d3-bae0-0b19e5e807a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3be7a489-8098-540d-bf9d-af04ee7bcc91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512229} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0744bce-0ef4-5d83-bb98-f340a557f518" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b10f970-f0c0-5c1c-948f-9f92d7941265" :question/math-academy-id "q-46167" :question/problem "The curve $y=4{x}^{2}-24x+24$ can be written as $y=a(x+b)^{2}+c.$ Calculate $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "To express the equation of the parabola in vertex form, we need to complete the square. \n\nFirst, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-24x+24 & =4({x}^{2}-6x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+6 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+6 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+6 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2})-{3}^{2}+6 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+6 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+6 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}-6x+6) & = \\\\ 4((x-3)^{2}-3) & = \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,  $a+b+c=4+(-3)+(-12)=-11.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d1705b0-834b-5792-8c06-6d5fc22048b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448684} {:answer/id #uuid "b112adca-cb36-57f3-bd90-2e7a480434e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d1705b0-834b-5792-8c06-6d5fc22048b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448684} :answer-field/id #uuid "92ff8be7-384f-50f9-a9cc-07cf8d5eaf16" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449382} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af4bc898-dce0-5f3d-9d2f-6a7b8e8ffcf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448686} {:answer/id #uuid "b1aa0055-1d04-5645-beb9-c3d9eaaccc11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af4bc898-dce0-5f3d-9d2f-6a7b8e8ffcf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448686} :answer-field/id #uuid "1841f66d-8716-5ae9-9721-03af6375e921" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449383} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edf57af7-4b49-59d6-a5a9-3387654cc8df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448688} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcfac6c-2e42-52a0-9938-f9d8d1b51b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dcfac6c-2e42-52a0-9938-f9d8d1b51b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448689} :answer-field/id #uuid "d516701d-8778-510c-80b6-62bc3f31f92b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c6cc47f7-3bf2-54bf-a0ea-9009aac01bb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47447" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$\n- **II.** $P-Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $4$\n- **III.** $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P+Q={x}^{3}+{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P-Q={x}^{3}-{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{7}={x}^{3+7}={x}^{10},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054812 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d06cd8-9aae-50ab-90dc-6c70b545d569" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Fractional Power Function Using the Power Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "004fb43a-8a3c-5d57-8932-47e0fe4aee15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512097} {:answer/id #uuid "4d853c4b-ff54-546b-bb02-2311564ce7a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512266} {:answer/id #uuid "c403ef6b-b382-5306-92d4-4c26b3f05c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512570} {:answer/id #uuid "dbafea93-c3c1-5c81-95ea-a42523faec59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512625} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d3b420-0896-5d50-a950-480cffd7a14e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbafea93-c3c1-5c81-95ea-a42523faec59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512625} :answer-field/id #uuid "2311da2e-4f69-5b66-a303-9fcae05b36b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "daf077a1-46a3-5bb7-a73f-ea41666a8c97" :question/math-academy-id "q-47831" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ when $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the radical as a rational exponent, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2/5}}={x}^{-2/5}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with $n=-\\frac{2}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2/5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{-2/5-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{-7/5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5{x}^{7/5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064272 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef1b856-e606-5faf-bc81-d441b2fbfdf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t)=E(t)-L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512128} {:answer/id #uuid "59459bbc-bff1-5a8f-9a50-e6ffa451c3d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=E(t)\\cdot L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512306} {:answer/id #uuid "a912d151-5821-56b2-bfc3-6a30d519da07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t)=E(t)+L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512502} {:answer/id #uuid "cafddae2-e99e-570b-af04-9b580b7a3489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=E(t)+L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512589} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d31616-58ba-5787-a9be-a1cfe9d0322c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=E(t)-L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1d31616-58ba-5787-a9be-a1cfe9d0322c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=E(t)-L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512684} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ee88502-7069-5e01-a8d0-c684a215ef26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c00a28ec-ae50-52ff-8f5b-35e535d7e4c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48923" :question/problem "To measure the success of an amusement park, Ed uses the following three functions that model the daily number of visitors to the park $t$ minutes after the park is opened to the public.\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of people inside the park\n\n- $E(t)$ - the rate at which people enter the park\n\n- $L(t)$ - the rate at which people leave the park\n\nWhat is the relationship between the functions above?" :question/worked-solution "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of people inside the park $t$ minutes after opening. Then,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}N(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }N}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${N}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of people inside the park changes $t$ minutes after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of people inside the park at time $t$ by finding the difference between the entry and exit rates at time $t.$ That is,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=E(t)-L(t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4a21cd-bd8d-5572-b932-76875827170a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Fibonacci Sequence Given in Sequence Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e2e40d7-3854-5e96-a861-a1a07ecb3a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512161} {:answer/id #uuid "8951bc5c-f431-5ff4-a335-7c7928becd8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512420} {:answer/id #uuid "b68ba403-6a1c-5a91-9565-64d68e9cdbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512533} {:answer/id #uuid "bf07614f-d15a-5062-80cc-4b44fbdc05d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512558} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ad6404-266a-54af-a78f-543ff14358ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf07614f-d15a-5062-80cc-4b44fbdc05d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512558} :answer-field/id #uuid "c54970a5-ea06-5ef6-84b8-80c8776bc786" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19fe87cd-0d79-5f77-b53f-849ad0d5ffc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-52557" :question/problem "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=1$ and ${a}_{2}=6,$ then ${a}_{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =6+1 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2+2} & ={a}_{2+1}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{3}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =7+6 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${a}_{4}=13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073917 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8b5ef8e-6f13-5496-ae7f-c9fbaf3e25c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Similarity Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09685bcf-87c8-5820-be62-2079ad8b2fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512123} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa03d95-5c1e-5462-baea-44a0db21983a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512198} {:answer/id #uuid "487920ed-c0d8-5fef-8da2-dbe125c0490e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512254} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f3412f-dd4f-5295-8d3f-906cebe4d2ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512475} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e819db-c3b6-5287-aeeb-bc588fb737a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aa03d95-5c1e-5462-baea-44a0db21983a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512198} :answer-field/id #uuid "49c9f005-3956-54dc-a98f-72829d84a02e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "384ee4e4-0b37-56f7-a3a5-86d23f2e43a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-53708" :question/problem "Which of the following transformations map any polygon to a similar polygon?\n\nI. Stretching with a stretch factor of $2$ in the $x$-direction and a stretch factor of $4$ in the $y$-direction\n\nII. Translation by $3$ units to the left and $4$ units upward\n\nIII. Dilation of scale factor $2$ with center $O,$ then reflection across the $y$-axis" :question/worked-solution "A similarity transformation is any transformation made up of rigid motions (rotations, reflections, and translations) and dilations.\n\nA similarity transformation will map any polygon to a similar polygon.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I includes stretching with different factors in the $x$- and $y$-direction. Thus, it is not a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation II is a combination of two translations, which are rigid motions. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation III is a combination of dilation of scale factor $2$ and a reflection. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df9158c-01a9-5596-9528-6033296b2925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Given Some Additional Information About the Geometric Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106793da-24f7-59ed-8b62-e13cfc57d52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512133} {:answer/id #uuid "571aa39e-400a-5863-b676-0015f3b249cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512297} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1bcd9e-18d9-5b81-bf60-b688fcd821e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512361} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa661ae-e5a1-55f6-8a14-031daabbb4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512364} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e88750-2d2a-5ed0-bb69-82c918766691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7e88750-2d2a-5ed0-bb69-82c918766691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512498} :answer-field/id #uuid "981ea52b-9e45-5858-a199-002953f70e37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "727e7006-93ff-5e5c-a29e-52adc22e5960" :question/math-academy-id "q-64010" :question/problem "The third term of a geometric sequence is $108$ and its fifth term is $3.$  Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive." :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{5}=3$ and ${a}_{3}=108.$ Substituting $n=5$ and $m=3$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{5-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{108} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192752 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1519905-a736-54a1-8778-cc03d019a084" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04b1019a-d92e-5d04-be9a-1a4844c6f0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512114} {:answer/id #uuid "706750fb-a8db-5883-9866-c48f23ec7c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512367} {:answer/id #uuid "767844f9-50f9-5d37-92c5-befa41f424fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512379} {:answer/id #uuid "82a50b38-647a-51f0-83dd-2e735fd413fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512404} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf7300d-5c9b-5c16-914a-b75e51c21227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecf7300d-5c9b-5c16-914a-b75e51c21227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512673} :answer-field/id #uuid "427c3f91-22fc-5c34-bf37-633a78d7c0ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c210cee-fd55-5399-a645-1646a3c5f4b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-64741" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={126}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{7\\pi }{10}$ is equivalent to ${126}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/89/89b5e2656378adb5d91c9f58c261e4beaae252b67819b904bf209f6ed8a5a25e.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{10}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{7\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$ must be negative because tangent (and therefore cotangent) is always negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083359 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06e51d62-ad2a-51c3-b276-eeb60022ab48" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Inverse Function Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48184947-7782-5f06-87ef-88301b445aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512251} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea8fc33-f0d3-5d44-843e-d1dc99182838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512273} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c00340-454e-506c-bf24-063b51dc5bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512526} {:answer/id #uuid "daf2acf4-7be1-5ff4-8dae-a4d6fffc11f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512622} {:answer/id #uuid "db256129-77ad-51ea-86d8-8bfe24ceef09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daf2acf4-7be1-5ff4-8dae-a4d6fffc11f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512622} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce61e97b-bb14-57d6-aa01-271d52a76ac5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1766a0cc-97ef-5939-9ca2-60e65363339c" :question/math-academy-id "q-66456" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=2x+6$ and $g(x)=\\frac{x}{2}+k.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have:\n\n$$\ng(f(x))=x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =\\frac{x}{2}+k \\\\ g(f(x)) & =\\frac{f(x)}{2}+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=2x+6$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =\\frac{f(x)}{2}+k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+6}{2}+k \\\\  & =x+3+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we also know that $g(f(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}+3+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ 3+k & =0 \\\\ k & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087692 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "169ca81a-c698-553c-9f47-d6c4303b82a9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Natural Logarithms of Fractions and Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6042c78b-71af-5276-80cf-ccdcb9284bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512329} {:answer/id #uuid "6614a67a-a7fe-55e2-be74-7e1ab3c9e3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512343} {:answer/id #uuid "b10534f0-4c95-531e-94d6-ba5e4a60964c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512516} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fff638-a832-599c-b20d-b7725b1e8582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512578} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b61c9c-6858-5b2e-a66c-1ae788e691c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6614a67a-a7fe-55e2-be74-7e1ab3c9e3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512343} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fd07e78-dd69-5881-8015-dd32bee64dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5359436-369e-5935-88c6-73a8053a9364" :question/math-academy-id "q-66526" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{1/2}}={e}^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-1/2})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-1/2})=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85558fe1-9c94-53cd-9b06-9d10fd915bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439873} {:answer/id #uuid "41bb42a2-237c-50d2-bde6-1e67a38a37e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41bb42a2-237c-50d2-bde6-1e67a38a37e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439874} :answer-field/id #uuid "c62a73cf-8c33-535d-9dea-0086d3e41c0e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440294} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dffdc7f5-2b92-5cae-91de-71f301a1f0da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439875} {:answer/id #uuid "528273a2-3a9d-5b9a-9b7a-08f3145a6a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dffdc7f5-2b92-5cae-91de-71f301a1f0da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439875} :answer-field/id #uuid "18169496-ec76-51fc-b481-2edec3fc6630" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440295} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce25a91-38a4-5e06-8dbc-24d243e66ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439877} {:answer/id #uuid "1ae5b007-91e6-5976-a3c2-72321bebd9a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ce25a91-38a4-5e06-8dbc-24d243e66ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439877} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5c6a5b6-c726-593a-9362-e5d7e8c23832" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "912ff428-058e-5197-af7a-fcbe7f3d6387" :question/math-academy-id "q-66785" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x-4|+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-\\infty ,3)$\n- **II.** $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-\\infty ,5]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-4|+2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $4$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-4|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x-4|$ by $2$ units up to get $f(x)=|x-4|+2.$\n\n![](images/56/56956d0f23ae6828098507712fd00525dc4f114a195716dc16e63d8475e9c143.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,3).$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in (1,\\infty )$  and $(-\\infty ,5].$ On each of these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087692 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "169ca81a-c698-553c-9f47-d6c4303b82a9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Natural Logarithms of Fractions and Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dc31748-131c-5761-b87b-12c70e2700fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512268} {:answer/id #uuid "8511cc21-378c-5a60-9221-6fb771890a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512407} {:answer/id #uuid "b219a9cf-83fc-5cad-a42c-df24894c4cbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512518} {:answer/id #uuid "f6bf7bc9-83ae-5185-89c3-ff3999355ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512698} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe0d590-58c4-5faf-8679-f3cf76a7cea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8511cc21-378c-5a60-9221-6fb771890a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512407} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1f049c7-00fd-5d21-8330-9fbecd737783" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f3a9a12-37bd-5534-bce9-24153ef1f5e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-66857" :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{e})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\sqrt[4]{e}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{e}={e}^{1/4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{e})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{1/4})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{1/4})=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{8}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083185 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fa847d9-f0bf-57fb-92b2-27c8a7cdb3b1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Even and Odd Functions Using the Algebraic Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56223238-871c-5059-a611-b22adabd18b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neither even nor odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512294} {:answer/id #uuid "73f077c3-f7dc-5e4b-b69b-312d30bce105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "It's not a function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512373} {:answer/id #uuid "888c316d-cb0b-54ba-954c-a843ead762fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512417} {:answer/id #uuid "d459b53f-1cde-5cb4-a70d-3e698301abf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512604} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d0aa2d-484b-5d54-b229-1c89a2a9149c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Both even and odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "888c316d-cb0b-54ba-954c-a843ead762fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512417} :answer-field/id #uuid "b597c4e2-567a-5468-a2f0-d74fa667be66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48b449a7-d277-5309-976b-1d1765d56b87" :question/math-academy-id "q-6690" :question/problem "Is the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-2x$ even, odd, or neither?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =2(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne f(x),$ the function is not even.\n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-(2{x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ the function is odd."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078471 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d979a7bd-42a1-5d4f-8717-1caa06ce623c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Index of a Term Given Two Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fe24ca2-15a6-51f1-a1df-7cc11fbacd48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410224} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd556ca-30b4-5288-8d72-abfec4b7f03d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410236} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef2bdba-9ddd-528c-9cea-425b4e82d718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410256} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d45a8f-d17e-5c84-8b87-5a64a5a6e762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410313} {:answer/id #uuid "debab0a7-4e2a-5e76-9d88-682a5f66970d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fe24ca2-15a6-51f1-a1df-7cc11fbacd48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410224} :answer-field/id #uuid "9596f751-f676-5a62-bf63-b8794255c731" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51d856f6-bec1-5419-b978-851c105050a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-67348" :question/problem "Given an arithmetic sequence that has ${a}_{4}=13$ and ${a}_{8}=41,$ what is the index of the term $90?$" :question/worked-solution "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term ${a}_{1}.$\n\nSince we are given ${a}_{4}=13$ and ${a}_{8}=41,$ we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{4}}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{41-13}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{4} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{4}=13$ is $3$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}+3d \\\\ 13 & ={a}_{1}+3(7) \\\\ 13 & ={a}_{1}+21 \\\\ -8 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-8+(n-1)(7) \\\\  & =-8+7n-7 \\\\  & =7n-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=90,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =7n-15 \\\\ 90 & =7n-15 \\\\ 105 & =7n \\\\ \\frac{105}{7} & =n \\\\ 15 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $90$ is the $15$th term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179649 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3db20b82-9561-5c54-8e12-8914bb6747c3" :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c23837-9722-5faa-902a-3e9a610ae018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4t}{3t(t+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512151} {:answer/id #uuid "389e3585-eb6e-5e0b-b7ad-c87d63aa1f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{t(t+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512223} {:answer/id #uuid "aaef2d17-80ab-59de-9959-f78c7423de66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t+3}{3t(t+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512503} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e2076f-e637-5540-becf-7c08397db46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6t+5}{t(t+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512662} {:answer/id #uuid "fd95737c-f9df-54a0-8ab8-9ef3e07fdf1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t+1}{3t(t+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd95737c-f9df-54a0-8ab8-9ef3e07fdf1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t+1}{3t(t+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512715} :answer-field/id #uuid "c848d579-8366-518a-a3ba-b24a64e2c36d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee038b53-750c-5b8c-99dc-a961b717435d" :question/math-academy-id "q-70358" :question/problem "$\\frac{t+1}{t(t+2)}-\\frac{1}{3t}=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $3t(t+2),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit.\n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $3$, and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $(t+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t+1}{t(t+2)}-\\frac{1}{3t} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{3}\\cdot \\frac{t+1}{t(t+2)}-\\frac{t+2}{t+2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(t+1)}{3t(t+2)}-\\frac{t+2}{3t(t+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(t+1)-(t+2)}{3t(t+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3t+3-t-2}{3t(t+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2t+1}{3t(t+2)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c8ba29-95c9-5494-be0b-9b67bee6e45c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining where a Tangent Line Intersects an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ed8aa18-f23e-5179-83b5-62f4300a01d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{5}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512274} {:answer/id #uuid "50ff8cf1-00c5-5be3-8b99-91abe28049cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512278} {:answer/id #uuid "63c9374b-d37c-56a0-a295-21e6bccf14d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512335} {:answer/id #uuid "76efb7a8-362e-53ed-a7a6-f85bdf02a95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512380} {:answer/id #uuid "b96971bd-8297-5518-aab6-d9a19486e8f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5}{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63c9374b-d37c-56a0-a295-21e6bccf14d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512335} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f3486a1-cfa1-570d-be9b-887c8a18ac0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43b75bde-30a1-5aa7-ad2b-9b63b76aaef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-70517" :question/problem "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=6\\sqrt{x}+5x-13$ at the point $(1,-2)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=6\\sqrt{x}+5x-13.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\sqrt{x}+5x-13) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{1/2}+5x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{-1/2}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=3(1)^{-1/2}+5=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =8(x-1) \\\\ y & =8x-8-2 \\\\ y & =8x-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =8x-10 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(\\frac{5}{4},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3173d361-b91d-5699-97d8-6595ef59d0a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e1a207-f9d0-53f3-b9be-3206f74c6704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512106} {:answer/id #uuid "992ff176-6fda-5a2c-8659-55baf90a83fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512458} {:answer/id #uuid "af6bec52-f2b1-55fa-890f-584e44e51b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512510} {:answer/id #uuid "c3294de8-305e-5066-b9e4-7eebd339a6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512565} {:answer/id #uuid "ea211255-4798-55dc-866b-aae486ca03e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "992ff176-6fda-5a2c-8659-55baf90a83fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512458} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5f55799-fa35-55be-b5e7-23ed06c306d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a33798d-52c0-57f6-a233-6585edd299f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-72754" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4ce20d9d732816efda0a2fd3aebabc467f9f2c73d0b6037f4ad8f95d8e8ef1e7.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/6e/6e070d26915bcb11118f5a23f36b80038d5dd865e32a8517a12c10939ca02f0b.png)\n\nFirst, we find the area of $△ABD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{85}^{∘} \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{85}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈23.908\\,7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area of $△BCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BCD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 14\\cdot 16\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{36.4}^{∘} \\\\  & =112\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{36.4}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈66.462\\,9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BCD} \\\\  & ≈23.908\\,7+66.462\\,9 \\\\  & ≈90.371\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $90.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054673 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "353c5ce4-024c-5c23-a226-99849fba18b4" :knowledge-point/title "Further Computing Probabilities Using Combinations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16176054-57d8-50dd-83a1-0a1e8ea37105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512150} {:answer/id #uuid "1ec32d6a-e0a8-5c9e-973c-c722de870f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512162} {:answer/id #uuid "240ee738-741b-5618-af59-6d108f123836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512178} {:answer/id #uuid "3be7b83f-658e-5186-bf99-aa39df89587a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512230} {:answer/id #uuid "40693834-30ab-5f7b-b4a8-410b121d0822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ec32d6a-e0a8-5c9e-973c-c722de870f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512162} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3a0c2d2-5b3a-522b-a6d4-7655774ccad3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7f9805c-ce96-515d-891a-332594997042" :question/math-academy-id "q-85920" :question/problem "A team of $5$ players is to be chosen at random from $7$ boys and $5$ girls.  What is the probability that $2$ boys and $3$ girls will be chosen? Round your answer to the nearest percent." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ represent the event described in the question. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{n}{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of ways to choose $5$ people out of a total of $12,$ and $n$ is the number of picks where $2$ are boys and $3$ are girls.\n\nSince the order in which the players are chosen does not matter, we use combinations.\n\nFirst, we compute $N.$ The number of ways to pick $5$ people from a total of $12$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N={}_{12}{C}_{5} & =\\frac{12!}{5!\\cdot 7!}=792.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $n.$ The computation of $n$ can be divided into two steps: \n\n- the number of ways to choose $2$ (out of $7$) boys is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{2}=\\frac{7!}{2!\\cdot 5!}=21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the number of ways to choose $3$ (out of $5$) girls is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{3!\\cdot 2!}=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the results from the above steps, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n={}_{7}{C}_{2}\\cdot {}_{5}{C}_{3}=21\\cdot 10=210.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{210}{792}≈0.27=27%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e08de2a-a2d3-5bb5-9eff-7650cc85dd48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512320} {:answer/id #uuid "95a56734-4623-5a29-823a-2dcc5eb112ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512451} {:answer/id #uuid "a3148c07-b30e-5ef9-a7ce-98aeae65443e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512480} {:answer/id #uuid "dd724d2d-9deb-58d6-b8f5-0793d0b3ea1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512631} {:answer/id #uuid "e559e8d5-044b-536f-be6b-c537f919870e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3148c07-b30e-5ef9-a7ce-98aeae65443e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512480} :answer-field/id #uuid "da5f6c2e-8aef-51f4-af0b-4f5580c1b351" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f70c750-2bdb-5272-875c-7d304eb180d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-88472" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}=3{e}^{5x}-4x{e}^{5x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x} & =3{e}^{5x}-4x{e}^{5x} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}-3{e}^{5x} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}+4x-3){e}^{5x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+4x-3=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{5x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}+4x-3=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x+3)(2x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{5x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{5x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},$ and their product is $(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "54d9d8e4-c521-5b7d-bca7-f3c5b0d2b1d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Magnitude of a Complex Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e8a0d3-e9d0-5d51-a265-c0563c9b3cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512186} {:answer/id #uuid "6862586c-2f33-53f0-a4a2-03c505c418b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512349} {:answer/id #uuid "8886309f-692e-500f-998c-a9b714ef0d0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512416} {:answer/id #uuid "e5742b23-5b17-5bb0-9ed8-9c567870fcff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512648} {:answer/id #uuid "faf87d00-4e4f-5040-a1bb-183235d0017e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6862586c-2f33-53f0-a4a2-03c505c418b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512349} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e3a5638-9657-57b0-b3fc-98e2b3a3e4f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d30493c3-51f6-5c83-b546-665bbdac5511" :question/math-academy-id "q-889" :question/problem "Find $|z|$ if $z=2-3\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a complex number $z=a+b\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFor the complex number $z=2-3\\text{i},$ we have $a=2$ and $b=-3.$ So, the magnitude is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}|2-3\\text{i}| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d059c56-ae7f-5fb6-8bc6-2ce97bb7f876" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function With an Even Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2126c568-9edc-5343-a977-03add492c720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512169} {:answer/id #uuid "70637956-2895-5e59-9c04-34a4660e4308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512366} {:answer/id #uuid "87d28730-c006-5e79-9d89-80cc3de93d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512415} {:answer/id #uuid "a6248731-39c4-573a-bae9-7c33dde12c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512493} {:answer/id #uuid "dfca70aa-c78a-5ad6-9130-294f9083ee33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6248731-39c4-573a-bae9-7c33dde12c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512493} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a6695ee-b357-51dc-b26f-ab87cc005be3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aebcd2ff-63a9-5afc-9df9-bf4ebc2f269f" :question/math-academy-id "q-93100" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=3\\sqrt[6]{3x+13}-6.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n3\\sqrt[6]{3x+13}-6=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt[6]{3x+13}-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\sqrt[6]{3x+13} & =6 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{3x+13} & =2 \\\\ (\\sqrt[6]{3x+13})^{6} & ={2}^{6} \\\\ 3x+13 & =64 \\\\ 3x & =51 \\\\ x & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt[6]{3\\cdot 17+13}-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\sqrt[6]{64}-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2-6 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=17.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0ab12b8-b1c9-5192-91be-cebc968d9587" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Logarithm Without a Calculator Using the Change of Base Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154fa8da-d8d4-5d35-a927-4d997f30031e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512146} {:answer/id #uuid "22ffff4a-deb5-521a-9391-9c312d39acfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512174} {:answer/id #uuid "29fbd695-065d-59f9-a978-64ab959589fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512195} {:answer/id #uuid "c582451e-1fd0-5671-8d95-3e20037da447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512575} {:answer/id #uuid "c67bb952-4b1f-5631-b6cd-9cff40172e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c67bb952-4b1f-5631-b6cd-9cff40172e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512577} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4740df2-7f5f-55f7-996f-1be23039010f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9bbddd9a-4e6f-51fe-9ece-2d2a83dcd62f" :question/math-academy-id "q-956" :question/problem "Calculate ${log}_{8}⁡(32)$ without using a calculator." :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ and $X=32.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡(32) & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(32)}{{log}_{2}⁡(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{5})}{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064186 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b514d4ef-68ad-5fd4-b802-e61ca86aec2c" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Equations With Radicals Into Logarithmic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "239c326a-0a3b-5c29-850c-cac8919a3673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{1/4}⁡16=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512177} {:answer/id #uuid "4779f4c7-29b4-5acc-9108-58e66d5a7ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{1/4}⁡2=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512249} {:answer/id #uuid "4794e66d-dfd4-5289-9730-730f5a69be16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{16}⁡2=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512250} {:answer/id #uuid "5e21fa3f-1c6c-5a3a-b2e9-4f260f1443e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡2=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512321} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e5741e-9798-5186-9c1e-7f4c4d89acfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡16=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4794e66d-dfd4-5289-9730-730f5a69be16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{16}⁡2=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512250} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c2319c-52ab-5a4c-97b1-53dcd4e20292" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32b42bfe-7421-54f2-832b-a9a8c0cbc5ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-99487" :question/problem "$\\sqrt[4]{16}=2$ is equivalent to" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given equation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n{16}^{1/4}=2\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${16}^{1/4}=2$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=2,b=16$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${log}_{16}⁡2=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}]}
